n into small manageable  chunks and you end up not doing anything  well guess what done is better than  perfect this is something that a lot of  people  have said in the past but it is so so so  true for years with this channel that  I've had just based around programming  I've constantly had these certain  expectations that I have to meet I can't  write this code I can't release this  feature in this current state people are  going to want to expect it to be the  perfect implementation the fastest  implementation the most efficient  implementation I hate all of that and I  am completely against all of that I  think this whole done is better than  perfect mentality is so important for  software engineering you're not going to  write the perfect code as soon as you  dive in and start writing your code it's  just not gonna work that way you're  going to have to iterate over what  you've done just sit down and start  writing slow terrible code if that's all  you know how to do right now do it if  you continually just put off writing any  code for a specific system because you  don't know how to write that system  you're not gonna get anywhere when I  first started programming and I still  think one of the reasons why I learnt  the initial kind of basics of game  programming and programming in general  so quickly was because I I was just I  was like 16 17 years old I had just  started programming I had no idea how  anything worked in terms of like how how  game systems were implemented but I  didn't care I was sitting in English  class I had my laptop I had Java and I  was just like you know what I'm going to  try and make this kind of game or I'm  gonna try and implement this feature and  using the very small amount of  experience and knowledge that I'd had I  sat down and I just I worked out how to  do it using that subset of skills and  that's something that I feel is really  lost nowadays you see all these people  on the internet putting up amazing  looking you know demo isn't like amazing  like feeling games or like incredible  renders of like their own art or  whatever and you just think wow like I  can't compete with that you know I have  to you know they went to college for  like three years or they've worked for  five years in the games industry I can't  do that right now wrong just do it man  stop comparing yourself to other people  stop carrying what  other people think of you just do it  because guess what if you can just make  this thing that you want to make and  it's on the screen and it's there You've  you've already beaten all other  expectations that you would never finish  it or that you can't do it because  you've done it sure it might not run in  one millisecond maybe it runs the ten  milliseconds but guess what that's still  fine and the thing is now that you've  implemented that you've got something on  the screen you can iterate over that you  can go back and you can work out how to  do it faster but you've done it you can  show people and you can it's amazing so  anyway what I did this week was I didn't  let myself kind of make excuses for ah I  should probably maybe think about this  video a little bit more before I do it I  should probably plan it a little bit  more I should probably you know I've got  these other things that I have to do I  can't make this video no I just said you  know what stop it you're gonna sit down  you're gonna hit record on the camera  and you're just going to make this video  and the amazing thing is once you start  doing it it just it all just comes to  you trust me you guys have no idea how  often I put off making videos if if  every time I was like oh man I want to  make a video if I actually made a video  every time I had a feeling of making a  video before I started doubting myself  for doing all that stuff you guys would  probably be getting like two videos a  day honestly and that's a huge thing for  me that I've been working on and that I  really want to improve going forward  this whole aspect of you know if you  kind of just actually do the work  instead of thinking that you're not good  enough or that you need to do XYZ before  you actually get up to the ability to do  a certain task that's going to go a long  way anyway what's up guys my name is the  Cherno welcome back to another one of  these like hazel dev vlog slash random  vlog Amanda's channel is really going  down here I thought about these videos  as a whole and thought that it would be  cool for these weekly vlogs to actually  have some kind of theme or some kind of  thought or maybe even like revolve  around a question from you guys  something like that that we could  discuss instead of just you know showing  you guys what I've done with  throughout the week because sometimes  there's not much to show and sometimes I  think it would be really cool to connect  a little bit closer with the community  and find out what you guys are up to as  well but to be honest I have had a  pretty productive week over the past  couple days I have been working on  implementing like Mouse picking into  hazel so that we can basically just  click on different objects in our scene  I'll show you guys what that looks like  in a minute I honestly have been really  motivated this week which i think is is  good and part of the reason I think is  that you know Hazel's finally getting to  the point where you know instead of just  laying foundational kind of boring stuff  I'm actually able to potentially start  using it very soon to actually make  stuff and it's kind of well basically  it's just gotten more fun and then on  the flip side with the actual game  engine series and and all that stuff you  know we're getting to the point where  people have started making a lot of  content for hazel which is really cool  and now I'm working on this little game  for hazel as well there's part of the  game engine series there's just so much  fun stuff going around very exciting  times but anyway without further ado  let's jump in and take a look at what  I've done in hazel this week so inside  our test scene and we kind of talked  about this last time a little bit with  the materials and all the other stuff  that I added to kind of create some sort  of workflow between blender or any 3d  modeling program really and hazel this  current scene as I showed you guys we  have kind of two two things that make up  the scene we have this kind of test  entity and there's no real entity  component system in hazel yet that's one  of the biggest next steps that I'm  actually going to take but at the moment  we have this gun entity which is this  animated mesh that we have here is just  a very basic test and we're just testing  animation as well as being able to have  multiple cord entities in the scene but  then this test entity mesh is the actual  scene what you see here and as you can  see it's made up of various sub meshes  and I think I accidentally exported the  sunlight from blend those which is why  we have Sun here as well but we have  floor cube cone sphere wall that's what  you see here being rendered as part of  our test scene now each of these are in  fact separate meshes this is the same  this is like the mesh hierarchy and they  do have various like transforms and all  of that stuff they are basically  independent objects and meshes that just  grouped into one one larger mesh and so  over the past week or really over the  past couple days what I've done is I've  made it so that you  can actually click and select various  sub meshes that make up your scene and  then manipulate them using these gizmos  so in other words I can click on the  sphere and then I can move it somewhere  else  like I can click on the corn and move it  I can scale it you know I can basically  start to create some kind of scene if I  really wanted to based off of just this  mesh that I've kind of imported here and  there's even snapping if you hold ctrl  which is kind of useful this is really  like early days and I expect to really  iterate over this probably quite a bit  but it's cool to see basically the  beginning of these these building blocks  I guess inside hazel dev if I move over  here I can move the cube somewhere else  maybe scale it up you can even rotate it  so the next real step would be to import  multiple assets because at the moment we  obviously just have the one kind of test  scene and you can load anything you want  by just clicking here and then you know  it'll open an open file dialog and you  can load any FBX in but you can only  still see one and of course you can't  really compose your scene from multiple  meshes and multiple entities so that's  kind of the biggest next step I guess in  all of this and of course if you at the  moment if you do select everything it  actually selects the entity you can see  I can like scale it up and down and like  rotate it and all that and then I can  obviously adjust everything from within  that kind of entity as well so we kind  of have like an entity transform and  then obviously like some kind of local  transform for each of these meshes  within that other things I still need to  work on other visuals for when you  select something some other words the  kind of visual feedback you get for  what's currently selected at the moment  is just simply its bounding box which is  just a very temporary thing because we  had bounding boxes anyway but we're  gonna probably likely want some kind of  outline shader maybe something that will  just create like an outline effect over  the currently selected object a lot of  3d programs do that blender does that  unity does that we'll probably go with  something like that we can also like do  a quick like a little wireframe pulse  when you first select an object just for  visual effect that might be nice a few  different ideas here and there of course  but right now you get the bounding box  and speaking of bounding boxes if you're  interested in how this is implemented  basically we just cast array from the  mouse into 3d space and then what we do  is we basically just query all the  objects in the scene  they're bounding box so in other words  all of these sub measures have a  bounding box that's calculated when you  actually import the mesh we then check  to see if the Ray collides with any of  these bounding boxes and if it does  collide with the bounding box for let's  just say this sphere for example we pick  the closest collision as well so in  other words there's this floor behind  the sphere but since the Ray hits the  sphere first if I click on this fear  we're inside the spheres bounding box so  now we just retrieve all the triangles  that make up the sphere and we test  those with the Ray and that gives you  very very precise intersections so in  other wise you can see if I click  literally like 1 pixel off here it's  gonna select the floor but if I click  here it's gonna select the sphere so  it's extremely precise you can see it  you can see that happening right here  which is really nice now most of this  was actually written in the livestreams  that I did yesterday I actually ended up  doing two live streams one in the  morning one in the afternoon because I  really wanted to get this finished so  you guys can definitely check that out  if you have access to that through  patreon but they definitely are still  some optimizations that can be done one  of which is for example at the moment we  just get the bounding box of every  single sub mesh but it will definitely  help to have a bounding box for the  entire entity that we can check first  because if we have like a more realistic  scene made up of like a lot of different  entities in the scene then checking  every single sub mesh and the entire  scene is gonna be very slower so instead  of that we can just check the entity  bounding box first kind of early up if  if it doesn't if the Ray doesn't hit  that and then if it does hit the  entities bounding box check out the sub  mesh bounding box and if it does hit a  sub mesh then check out the triangles  but again obviously if you end up having  like a very very very detailed model  with like a million triangles and that  is gonna be quite slower so you could  just potentially use like a lower Lord  of that or something like that there's a  few different things you can do but that  is the basic idea  all right so I hope you guys enjoy that  will look into hazel Deb don't forget  that if you help support the channel and  hazel and all that stuff on patreon  account for Sasha Turner you will get  access to not only live streams the  Charmy working on this stuff you'll also  get access to all the source code for  what you see here which is hazel dev  thank you so much for your support let  me know what you guys thought of this  video love you all I will see you next  time goodbye  [Music]  [Music]
 

